The question asks whether a private parent method is not part of the inheritance contract, meaning a child class may declare a method with the same name and any visibility.

In PHP:
- Private methods are NOT part of the inheritance contract. They are specific to the class in which they are declared.
- A child class can indeed declare a method with the same name as a private method in the parent class, and it can have any visibility (public, protected, or private).
- This is because private methods are hidden from child classes, so the child's method is essentially a completely new method, not an override.

For example:
```php
class Parent {
    private function foo() { return 'parent'; }
}
class Child extends Parent {
    public function foo() { return 'child'; }
}
```
This is perfectly valid in PHP. The private method in the parent is invisible to the child, so there's no conflict.

So the statement is TRUE.

